      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "THE MECHANISM MATCH\nSurge needs to interpolate a continuous value (price) across discrete cells — a gradient, not a lookup table. That only\nworks if every neighbor is the same distance away, so \"one step\" means one consistent thing in every direction. Squares\nfail this by construction (edge- vs. corner-neighbors differ by √2); hexagons satisfy it by construction (6 neighbors,\none shared-edge distance). H3 doesn't make pricing smoother by being cleverer about zones — it changes the shape of\nspace so smoothing becomes arithmetic.",
      "fontSize": 12.5,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": "b_mech",
      "originalText": "THE MECHANISM MATCH\nSurge needs to interpolate a continuous value (price) across discrete cells — a gradient, not a lookup table. That only\nworks if every neighbor is the same distance away, so \"one step\" means one consistent thing in every direction. Squares\nfail this by construction (edge- vs. corner-neighbors differ by √2); hexagons satisfy it by construction (6 neighbors,\none shared-edge distance). H3 doesn't make pricing smoother by being cleverer about zones — it changes the shape of\nspace so smoothing becomes arithmetic.",
      "lineHeight": 1.3
    },
    {
      "id": "e_footnote",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 30,
      "y": 635,
      "width": 1140,
      "height": 65,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#555555",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "roundness": null,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1752969600000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The tradeoff didn't disappear — it moved to the globe itself. You cannot tile a sphere with only hexagons: Euler's formula\nforces exactly 12 pentagon cells into existence at every resolution, each with 5 neighbors instead of 6, breaking the exact\nuniformity everywhere else. Uber's fix wasn't mathematical, it was cartographic: orient the icosahedron (per Buckminster\nFuller's projection) so all 12 land in open ocean, nowhere near a city anyone is pricing a ride in.",
      "fontSize": 12,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The tradeoff didn't disappear — it moved to the globe itself. You cannot tile a sphere with only hexagons: Euler's formula\nforces exactly 12 pentagon cells into existence at every resolution, each with 5 neighbors instead of 6, breaking the exact\nuniformity everywhere else. Uber's fix wasn't mathematical, it was cartographic: orient the icosahedron (per Buckminster\nFuller's projection) so all 12 land in open ocean, nowhere near a city anyone is pricing a ride in.",
      "lineHeight": 1.3
    }
  ],
